AlertU for Fedora
==================

Which releases this package installs on
----------------------------------------

Fedora 40 or newer. The glibc floor is GLIBC_2.39, measured from the
`GLIBC_*` symbol versions the four binaries actually reference (`rpm -qp
--requires`, resolved by `--auto-req` against `find-requires`), not asserted
by hand — that number happens to match the `.deb`'s `libc6 (>= 2.39)` floor,
but the two are reached by different mechanisms: the deb's is a single
package-version floor taken from its build toolchain, this one is the highest
individual symbol version any binary links against. Fedora 39 and older will
fail to install. Build from source there.

If you installed manually before
---------------------------------

The manual instructions put a unit in /etc/systemd/system and binaries in
/usr/local/bin. Both take precedence over this package's — the unit shadows
/usr/lib/systemd/system/alertu-daemon.service, and /usr/local/bin comes before
/usr/bin on PATH — so the package looks installed while the old build keeps
running. `dnf remove` removes neither. Clear them out before installing:

    sudo systemctl disable --now alertu-daemon
    sudo rm -f /etc/systemd/system/alertu-daemon.service
    sudo rm -f /usr/local/bin/alertu-daemon /usr/local/bin/alertu-ctl
    rm -f ~/.local/bin/alertu-gui ~/.local/bin/alertu-settings
    rm -f ~/.config/systemd/user/alertu-gui.service

What the package grants the daemon
----------------------------------

It installs /usr/share/polkit-1/rules.d/49-alertu.rules, which lets the `alertu`
system user lock and unlock your graphical session without authenticating.

Without that rule nothing locks. The daemon is not you, so `loginctl
lock-session` is the privileged action org.freedesktop.login1.lock-sessions, and
systemd ships it as auth_admin_keep for every subject: arming would fail with
"Interactive authentication required" and the screen would stay up.

logind describes that action as "Lock or unlock active sessions" and offers no
separate action for locking alone, so the grant necessarily covers unlocking as
well — which is what disarming needs anyway. The consequence is worth stating:
anyone who achieves code execution as `alertu` can unlock your screen. Delete the
file to withdraw the grant; arming stops working at the same time.

Three things this package deliberately does not do.
----------------------------------------------------

It does not start the daemon.
------------------------------

`%post` runs `systemctl preset alertu-daemon.service` rather than
`systemctl enable --now`, because honouring the administrator's preset policy
is the Fedora convention — a maintainer script that force-enables a unit
overrides a choice the administrator may have made on purpose. Fedora's
default preset policy ends with `disable *`, so on a stock system this leaves
the daemon installed but not running. This is a deliberate divergence from
the `.deb`, which does start its daemon. Start it yourself:

    sudo systemctl enable --now alertu-daemon

It does not force the tray on either.
-------------------------------------

`%post` runs `systemctl --global preset alertu-gui.service`, the same preset
convention one scope out: `--global` covers systemd user instances started after
that point, so it needs no access to the user instance of whoever is logged in.
Under Fedora's default policy that leaves the tray disabled, like the daemon.
This is where the `.deb` diverges — it enables the tray outright. Turn it on for
your account with:

    systemctl --user enable --now alertu-gui

It does not add you to the `alertu` group — but the settings window offers to.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

The daemon's control socket is mode 0660, owned by `alertu`. Anyone who can
connect to it can disarm the alarm, read the configuration including the
webhook URL, and redirect the paths handed to the helper programs. Group
membership is therefore a privilege grant, not a convenience.

A package still cannot grant it: it does not know which account is yours, and
RPM scriptlets have no business modifying existing accounts non-interactively.
What it can do is make the grant one click instead of one command. Open the
settings window; if this account cannot reach the daemon, it offers "Authorize
this account", which runs

    pkexec /usr/lib/alertu/alertu-authorize

behind one password prompt. That helper takes no arguments and reads the account
to add from PKEXEC_UID, so it can only ever add its own caller — a helper that
accepted a username would be a password-protected "add anyone to the group that
commands the alarm", which is a worse deal than the plain `usermod` it replaces.

Log out and back in afterwards: a process carries the groups it started with, so
nothing already running picks the membership up. `sudo usermod -aG alertu <user>`
still works if you would rather do it yourself.

It does not ship a configuration file.
--------------------------------------

The daemon rewrites its configuration whenever you change a setting from the
tray, the settings window or `alertu-ctl set-config`. A file under /etc would
be an RPM-tracked config file, and `dnf` would flag it as modified on every
upgrade once the daemon had touched it.

Instead the package creates /etc/alertu/ empty and lets the daemon write
config.toml there itself. A commented example is in this directory. The
daemon starts on built-in defaults, with no remote configured, and says so in
its log.

Removing the package
---------------------

`dnf remove` spares /etc/alertu — nothing under it is owned by the package,
so removal leaves your configuration in place.

It also spares /var/lib/alertu/snapshots. Those are webcam stills from alarms
you may still want. Remove both by hand if you don't.
